====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To exercise authoritative [[control]] over; to tell (someone) what to do, often repeatedly.
#: {{syn|en|lord over|boss around}}
#* '''1931''', Robert L. May, ''Rudolph'', ''The Red-Nosed Reindeer'', Montgomery Ward (publisher):
#*: By YOU last night’s journey was actually '''bossed'''<!--.--> / Without you, I’m certain, we’d all have been lost.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1932|author=Lorine Pruette|title=The Parent and the Happy Child|page=76
|passage=His sisters '''bossed''' him and spoiled him. All their lives he was to go on being their little brother, who could do no wrong, because he was the baby; [...]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1967|author=Pelham Grenville Wodehouse|title=The purloined paperweight|page=90
|passage=She '''bossed''' him, and he's never gotten over it. She still orders him around, and instead of telling her to go soak her head, he just says 'Yes, ma'am' as weak as a newborn jellyfish [...]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=Jean Toomer|title=The wayward and the seeking: a collection of writings by Jean Toomer|page=40
|passage=For if, on the one hand, I '''bossed''' him and showed him what to do and how to do it, [...]}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=June 14 2021|work=BBC Sport|author=Scott Mullen|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/51197776|title=Scotland 0-2 Czech Republic|passage=Clarke was undoubtedly made to change things and for spells, the Czechs '''bossed''' the game. Scotland's midfield was nullified, Dykes struggled to hold the ball up while, barring a small handful of forays by Robertson, there was a distinct lack of width from the Scots.}}

